🧩 What a Singleton is
It’s a design pattern that ensures:

-There’s only one instance of a class, ever.
-Everyone who needs it gets a reference to that same instance.

You can think of it like:
“One counter that all parts of the app share.”

💭 How it usually works
Three core steps — you’ll recognize these when you go back to your code:

1. Private constructor
→ so no one can create new instances with new Counter()

2. Private static variable holding “the one instance”
→ this is the actual singleton

3. Public static method (often called getInstance())
→ gives you access to that one shared instance

🔄 Applied to your situation

In your code, you currently create a Counter by doing new Counter().
But that means multiple counters could exist, each with its own value.

The singleton pattern fixes that so everyone shares the same value.
That’s especially useful for something like a counter — imagine a global score, total clicks, or ID generator.

