Med static counter feltvariabel uden instantiering af objektet:
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
You can’t later subclass or extend Counter’s behavior:
-----------------------------------------------------
public class FancyCounter extends Counter {
    public static void resetToTen() {
        value = 10;
    }
}
Then FancyCounter.value is actually a completely separate static variable from Counter.value
(Java’s static members are bound to the class, not inherited per instance).
So Counter.value and FancyCounter.value would drift apart → not shared.
✅ With a Singleton object, subclasses could inherit and override instance methods,
because there’s a real object with runtime polymorphism.
-----------------------------------------------------
You can’t implement interfaces like Comparable or Serializable:
-----------------------------------------------------
If everything’s static, there’s no instance to serialize or compare.
Static data isn’t serialized by default. Only objects are.
So again: the Singleton pattern gives you the object identity that interfaces expect.
So the Singleton pattern’s purpose is:
→ Give you one shared instance, but still keep it an object with real OOP behavior.
-----------------------------------------------------
You can’t pass Counter as an object to a method, because it’s not an instance.
-----------------------------------------------------
You can’t encapsulate how value changes (other classes could just say Counter.value = 9999; directly).
-----------------------------------------------------

✅ Exactly the point of a singleton:
The first time the program loads the Counter class, a single Counter object is created and stored in the static field instance.
Every time you later call Counter.getInstance(), you don’t make a new object — you just get a reference to that same one.

🧠 Why this distinction matters
For a simple case like your Counter, it makes no real difference — both behave identically after creation.
But for larger systems, if that singleton does something expensive (like connect to a database or load a huge file),
eager creation would waste resources if it’s never actually used.
That’s why “lazy” creation can be more memory- or performance-efficient.

